# Direction as a rule

### The README's arrows, kept as a test

Week 4, rung 3 of 7 (Layers), talk 2

---

## A diagram is L2

The README of rung 3 draws the layers and lists who may import what:

```
apps/cli ──► @ladder/gen ──► @ladder/model
    │                            ▲
    └──────► @ladder/lite ───────┘
```

A coding agent may read it, forget it, or decide that "just this once" `lite` can import a helper from
`gen`. `AGENTS.md` says it plainly: "The layering, the port rule and the README's Architecture section say
the same thing; the tests decide."

So each arrow climbs to L3, a test, with an L4 message.

---

## The table the rules read

```ts
// variants/rung-3-layers/tests/architecture/architecture.test.ts
/** Each layer, where it lives, the only workspace packages it may import, and why. */
const LAYERS: Record<string, { dir: string; may: string[]; job: string }> = {
  "@ladder/model": { dir: "packages/model", may: [], job: "model is the shared vocabulary of types; ..." },
  "@ladder/gen":   { dir: "packages/gen", may: ["@ladder/model"], job: "gen turns a course folder into a Course; ..." },
  "@ladder/lite":  { dir: "packages/lite", may: ["@ladder/model"], job: "lite turns a Course into HTML; ..." },
  "@ladder/cli":   { dir: "apps/cli", may: ["@ladder/model", "@ladder/gen", "@ladder/lite"], job: "cli is the composition root ..." },
};
```

Each layer carries its **reason**, the `job`. The reason goes into the failure, so whoever meets it learns
why, not just what.

---

## How the test reads code

```ts
/** Source without comments, so a comment may name what the code must not do. */
const stripComments = (text: string) => text.replace(/\/\*[\s\S]*?\*\/|(?<![:"'])\/\/.*$/gm, "");

const modules: Module[] = Object.entries(LAYERS).flatMap(([layer, { dir }]) =>
  readdirSync(join(ROOT, dir, "src"), { recursive: true, encoding: "utf8" })
    .filter((file) => file.endsWith(".ts"))
    .map((file) => ({ layer, file: `${dir}/src/${file}`, source: stripComments(readFileSync(...)) })));

const specifiers = (source: string) =>
  [...source.matchAll(/(?:from|import)\s*\(?\s*"([^"]+)"/g)].map((match) => match[1]!);
```

Plain text and regular expressions, with `node:test`. No parser and no dependency: enough for four
packages whose imports are all `import ... from "..."`.

---

## Four rules about direction

| Rule | Checks | Applies to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Layering | workspace imports are in the layer's `may` list | every file |
| Package boundary | a relative import never leaves its own `src/` | every file |
| Model | only `export type` and `export interface`; no imports | `model` |
| Closure | `package.json` declares exactly the `@ladder/*` packages the source imports | every package |

Two more rules, Port and Honest Double, guard the edges. They are next week's.

---

## A teaching failure: layering

`packages/lite/src/site-builder.ts` gains `import { parseMarkdown } from "@ladder/gen";`

```
not ok 10 - packages/lite/src/site-builder.ts
  Layering rule: @ladder/lite may import only [@ladder/model], because lite turns a Course into HTML;
  it never reads a course folder, so it can render any tutors.json. Move the code to a layer that may
  make this import, or take what you need as an argument; apps/cli wires the layers together
  (README.md, Architecture).
    packages/lite/src/site-builder.ts imports @ladder/gen
not ok 15 - each package.json declares exactly the workspace packages its source imports
  Closure rule: ... Add the missing dependency as "workspace:*", or remove the unused one, ...
    packages/lite/package.json declares [@ladder/model] but its src imports [@ladder/gen, @ladder/model]
```

The rule, the offender, the reason, and the ways out. That is L4.

---

## Two rules, two fixes: which wins?

The Closure rule offers "add the missing dependency". That turns Closure green and leaves Layering red.

- Closure is about **consistency**: the manifest and the imports agree.
- Layering is about **intent**: this arrow should not exist.

When two rules disagree about the fix, the one that names the **reason** wins. Here `lite` does not need
`parseMarkdown` at all: the `Course` it receives already has titles.

And `pnpm test` stops even earlier, at `tsc -p packages/lite`: `TS2307: Cannot find module '@ladder/gen'`.
pnpm links into a package only what it declares.

---

## The other two, failing

```
Model rule: packages/model holds only `export type` and `export interface`, and imports nothing, so it
adds no code and no dependency to any layer. Put functions and constants in the layer that uses them
(preOrder and folderKinds live in gen).
    packages/model/src/index.ts: export const LO_TYPES = ["course", "topic"];

Package boundary rule: a relative import may not leave packages/lite/src. Import another package by its
name (@ladder/...) through its index.ts, and only if the layering rule allows it.
    packages/lite/src/site-builder.ts imports "../../gen/src/file-system-port.ts"
```

The Layering rule is silent on the second: it reads only `@ladder/` names. That gap is why the boundary rule
exists.

---

## A cycle, caught three ways

`packages/model/src/index.ts` gains `import type { FileSystemPort } from "@ladder/gen";`

```
Layering rule: @ladder/model may import only [], because model is the shared vocabulary of types; ...
    packages/model/src/index.ts imports @ladder/gen
Model rule: packages/model holds only `export type` and `export interface`, and imports nothing, ...
Closure rule: ... packages/model/package.json declares [] but its src imports [@ladder/gen]
# fail 3
```

`gen` already imports `model`, so this is a cycle: neither package can change alone. Upstream's rule is
`no-cross-package-cycle` in `.dependency-cruiser.cjs`: "A cycle that leaves a workspace and comes back makes
the layering meaningless; invert one edge through a seam."

---

## Lean: coupling builds in handoffs

| If this arrow existed | The waste it would create |
| --- | --- |
| `lite` → `gen` | every change to `gen` is a **handoff** to `lite`: relearn, re-test, wait |
| `model` → `gen` | a **cycle**: neither package can be released or tested alone; work waits on work |
| a relative import into `gen/src` | **relearning**: a private helper becomes public by accident, and its next change breaks a stranger |
| a runtime helper in `model` | **extra features** in the one package everybody depends on |

Each direction rule removes one of these before it happens. The teaching failure removes another waste:
**relearning** why the rule exists, every time someone meets it.

---

## The Human Boundary around the rules

```markdown
<!-- variants/rung-3-layers/AGENTS.md, "Ask a human first" -->
- Adding or upgrading any dependency, or changing `tests/rung-fence.test.ts`.
- Changing `tests/architecture/`, a package's `tsconfig.json`, or the port's methods.
- Adding a package or app, or changing this file.
```

An agent that meets the Layering rule changes the code. Only a person changes `LAYERS`. Next week's
assignment is exactly such a change: a new package, with you, not the agent, deciding where it sits.

---

## Upstream: dependency-cruiser

```js
// .dependency-cruiser.cjs (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
const LAYERS = [
  { name: "foundation", paths: ["packages/jsr/[^/]+", "packages/svelte/utils/logger", "packages/svelte/utils/metrics"] },
  { name: "core", paths: ["packages/svelte/runes", "packages/svelte/course", ...] },
  ...
];
const layerRules = LAYERS.slice(0, -1).map((layer, index) => ({
  name: `layer-${layer.name}`,
  comment: `The ${layer.name} layer must not import from a layer above it (...). See the Architecture section of README.md.`,
  from: { path: anyOf(layer.paths) },
  to: { path: anyOf(LAYERS.slice(index + 1).flatMap((l) => l.paths)) }
}));
```

Plus named rules such as `no-app-to-app` and `no-cross-workspace-relative-import` (rung 3's Package
boundary rule). The same ideas, on a resolved module graph.

---

## Text rules or a graph?

| | Rung 3 (text and regex) | Upstream (dependency-cruiser) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Dependency | none | `dependency-cruiser` |
| Sees | `import ... from "..."` in source | the resolved module graph, `.svelte` included |
| Proves a rule can fail | the lab, by hand | negative fixtures in `tests/architecture/fixtures/workspace` |
| Old violations | none allowed | a ratchet, `tests/architecture/known-violations.txt` |

Rung 3's rule count is small enough for text. Upstream needs the graph. Both put the reason in the message.

---

## Summary

- the README's diagram is L2; the architecture test makes each arrow L3 with an L4 message
- the reason for each layer lives in `LAYERS`, so in every failure
- when two fixes compete, follow the rule that names the reason
- coupling is built-in handoff waste; direction rules remove it before it starts
- upstream keeps the same rules with dependency-cruiser

Read "Dependency direction", then break the layers in Lab 4.
